WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.128
In March 1868 a twenty-nine-year-old Scot landed in San

2
00:00:04.128 --> 00:00:08.304
Francisco, asked a passer-by for the quickest way out of the

3
00:00:08.304 --> 00:00:13.593
city, and set off east on foot toward a wall of mountains he had never seen.

4
00:00:13.943 --> 00:00:17.616
John Muir was born in Dunbar, Scotland, on 21 April

5
00:00:17.616 --> 00:00:21.722
1838, and attended the local schools until he was eleven.

6
00:00:22.072 --> 00:00:25.244
In 1849 his family emigrated to the United

7
00:00:25.244 --> 00:00:28.341
States and settled on Wisconsin farmland.

8
00:00:28.691 --> 00:00:31.906
In 1867, working in a carriage shop in

9
00:00:31.906 --> 00:00:36.644
Indianapolis, a file slipped and blinded him in one eye.

10
00:00:36.994 --> 00:00:39.171
When his sight came back he resolved to give up

11
00:00:39.171 --> 00:00:41.487
machinery and devote himself to the natural world.

12
00:00:41.837 --> 00:00:44.316
He walked out across the San Joaquin Valley and up into

13
00:00:44.316 --> 00:00:46.795
the Sierra Nevada, which he named "the Range of Light."

14
00:00:47.145 --> 00:00:50.210
Writing, for Muir, was a way of defending what he loved.

15
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.913
In 1890, after a campaign Muir fought alongside the editor Robert

16
00:00:54.913 --> 00:00:59.534
Underwood Johnson, an act of Congress created Yosemite National Park.

17
00:00:59.884 --> 00:01:03.106
Two years later Muir and a circle of allies founded the Sierra

18
00:01:03.106 --> 00:01:06.432
Club "to do something for wildness and make the mountains glad."

19
00:01:06.782 --> 00:01:09.435
President Theodore Roosevelt came west

20
00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:12.367
in 1903 to see Yosemite with Muir himself.

21
00:01:12.717 --> 00:01:16.290
The two slipped away from the official party into the back country

22
00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.809
for several days; Roosevelt went home and threw the weight of the

23
00:01:19.809 --> 00:01:23.653
presidency behind protecting the nation's forests, parks and monuments.

24
00:01:24.003 --> 00:01:24.839
Muir wrote:

25
00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:29.932
“Thousands of tired, nerve-shaken, over-civilized people are beginning to find

26
00:01:29.932 --> 00:01:34.367
out that going to the mountains is going home; that wildness is a necessity.”

27
00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:37.147
Which wild place near you would you fight to

28
00:01:37.147 --> 00:01:39.971
protect, and how would you persuade other people to help?
